{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792708868,"nonce":"fOuewqMIpk6LCAqFsAR9X+uc6+M=","message":"Fix in 9492505d: counted for loops keep an i64 counter for any test of the form i \u003c E or i \u003c= E (before, only i \u003c xs.length), and an index of the form counter +/- small constant is computed in integers, so the guard is an integer compare that the shared bounds_version pass can fold. NUC (x86-64), release, minimum of 5, whole-process time. Guard microbenchmark, 200M reads, provable index vs forced guard: before 0.38 s vs 0.51 s default tier and 0.11 s vs 0.28 s with --llvm; after the guarded loop matches the provable one (0.38 s, 0.11 s). Kernels, default tier / --llvm, before to after: spectral-norm 1000 0.16 to 0.09 s / 0.13 to 0.07 s (Node 0.11 s), k % 7 100M 0.35 to 0.20 s / 0.18 to 0.16 s (Node 0.15 s), sieve 20M 0.40 to 0.33 s / 0.28 to 0.26 s, fannkuch(10) 1.10 to 0.95 s / 0.55 to 0.50 s (Node 0.24 s). Fannkuch is therefore not mainly the guard: its counters are plain variables in while loops and its indexes are floating-point expressions (perm[r - 1], perm[j]) that still go through the float guard. Corpus unchanged at 922 PASS, local 628 pass.","files":null}]}